{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994450,"nonce":"jKS9gPn0Lq812j9RS4Nwb+INl7M=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrhIQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/206","origin":"github"},"title":"dd: a finite-difference subdomain that starts on a coefficient jump loses its convergence order","message":"When a finite-difference subdomain of `jno.dd.couple` begins exactly on a jump of the coefficient, the coupled solution loses its convergence order, although the overlap lies entirely in the uniform material. Moving the start of the finite-difference region a little into the uniform material restores second order.\n\nThis is the layout of `test_material_interface_via_overlap_and_kx` in `tests/test_domain_decomposition.py` (FEM on `x \u003c= 0.6`, finite differences on `x \u003e= 0.5`, jump at `x = 0.5`), which its docstring calls the robust way. The test asserts the interface value within 0.03 on one mesh, so it does not notice.\n\nReproduced on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22).\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe script of #205, with the last loop replaced by:\n\n```python\nfor fdm_start in (0.6, 0.5):\n    for h in (0.1, 0.05, 0.025):\n        err, info = run(h, fdm_start, 40)\n        print(f\"FDM from x = {fdm_start}  h = {h}: error {err:.3e}  overlap jump {info['overlap_jump']:.3e}\")\n```\n\n`-div(k grad u) = f` on the unit square, `k = 1` for `x \u003c 0.5` and `k = 10` beyond, a mesh line on the jump, FEM on `x \u003c= 0.7` with the jump in its coefficient, finite differences with the uniform `k = 10`. Relative L2 error at the nodes against the exact solution:\n\n| Mesh size | Finite differences from `x = 0.6` | Finite differences from `x = 0.5` (the jump) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.1 | 9.1e-03 | 1.7e-02 |\n| 0.05 | 2.0e-03 | 1.2e-02 |\n| 0.025 | 3.6e-04 | 7.3e-03 |\n| observed order | about 2.3 | about 0.6 |\n\nThe overlap jump tells the same story: 2.6e-03, 4.3e-04, 1.1e-04 against 3.7e-02, 2.8e-02, 1.7e-02. With the region starting on the jump the two subdomain solutions disagree in the overlap by percent.\n\n## Likely cause (not confirmed by a run)\n\nThe nodes on `x = 0.5` belong to the finite-difference region. If they are treated as its interior nodes, the strong-form stencil with the uniform coefficient is applied on the jump, where the equation it discretises does not hold (the flux condition is missing).\n\n## Possible fixes\n\n- Treat the nodes on the edge of a coupled region as receiving nodes, never as interior nodes of that region.\n- Or state in the coupling guide that a strong-form region must start inside the uniform material, and move the test's region accordingly.\n- Tighten the test: assert the error against the analytic profile on two mesh sizes.","files":null}]}